{#
  Native Kubernetes manifests for the Thread infrastructure, deployed via
  ArgoCD. Contains:
    - PersistentVolumeClaim    Longhorn volume for /data holding the OpenThread
                               Border Router state, including the active Thread
                               network dataset (network key, PAN ID). Losing it
                               means re-forming the Thread network and
                               re-commissioning every device.
    - DaemonSet                generic-device-plugin, which advertises the
                               ZBT-2 radio and /dev/net/tun to the kubelet as
                               allocatable resources (squat.ai/zbt2 and
                               squat.ai/tun).
    - Deployment               OpenThread Border Router (OTBR) driving the
                               Home Assistant Connect ZBT-2 radio over serial.
                               Built locally on the radio host with the
                               mDNSResponder mDNS backend so it port-shares
                               UDP 5353 with the Matter server's embedded mDNS
                               (the native OpenThread backend grabs 5353
                               exclusively and conflicts). See thread.yml.
    - PersistentVolumeClaim    Longhorn volume for the Matter server's /data,
                               holding the Matter fabric credentials and node
                               database. Losing it means re-commissioning
                               every Matter device.
    - Deployment               matterjs-server (Matter.js), the Matter
                               controller that Home Assistant's Matter
                               integration connects to over websocket. It
                               commissions and controls Matter devices over
                               IPv6; packets to Thread devices transit OTBR
                               like any routed traffic. Its mDNS sets
                               SO_REUSEPORT so it port-shares UDP 5353 with
                               the OTBR mDNSResponder on the same host.

  Security model — least privilege via device plugin:
  Kubernetes has no equivalent of Docker's `devices:` mapping, so the naive
  way to hand a pod a serial device is hostPath + privileged, which grants
  ALL capabilities, ALL host devices, and disables seccomp — far more than
  OTBR needs. Instead, the generic-device-plugin DaemonSet implements the
  kubelet device-plugin API: the OTBR pod requests the radio and tun devices
  through resources.limits, the kubelet adds exactly those two device-cgroup
  entries, and the OTBR container itself runs unprivileged with only the
  capabilities it actually uses:
    - NET_ADMIN  create the wpan0 tunnel interface, install ip6tables rules,
                 and send IPv6 router advertisements on the LAN
    - NET_RAW    raw ICMPv6 sockets for neighbor/router discovery
    - IPC_LOCK   mDNSResponder (mdnsd) memory locking
  The device plugin container itself must run privileged (it needs /dev and
  the kubelet's device-plugins socket — a documented requirement of the
  device-plugin API), but it is a small single-purpose binary with no
  network exposure, which is a much smaller attack surface than running the
  whole border router privileged. The IPv6 forwarding sysctls that a
  privileged OTBR would set itself are instead applied persistently on the
  radio host by thread.yml.

  The OTBR pod runs with hostNetwork because it routes IPv6 between the
  Thread mesh (wpan0) and the node's LAN interface and must advertise itself
  (mDNS, router advertisements) as a real LAN host. The Matter server also
  runs with hostNetwork: it commissions devices via mDNS/IPv6 on the real
  LAN, which does not work from behind the pod network. It needs no added
  capabilities — hostNetwork plus ordinary UDP sockets suffice.

  All workloads pin to the node labeled as the Thread radio host
  (thread_radio_host inventory flag): OTBR because the ZBT-2 serial device
  is local, and the Matter server because on the radio host the routes to
  the Thread mesh (via wpan0) exist directly in the host routing table —
  on any other node they would have to be learned from OTBR's router
  advertisements, which the other nodes' sysctls do not accept
  (accept_ra_rt_info_max_plen defaults to 0). The Recreate strategy
  prevents two pods from contending for the single radio and the
  ReadWriteOnce data volumes.

  ClusterIP Services front both control endpoints so in-cluster clients use
  stable DNS names instead of the radio host's IP: Home Assistant connects
  to http://otbr.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local:<rest-port> (OTBR
  integration) and ws://matter-server.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local:<matter-port>/ws
  (Matter integration). With hostNetwork pods the Service endpoints resolve
  to the node IP, so if the radio host ever changes, the Services follow the
  pods and Home Assistant's configuration stays valid. LAN clients (e.g.
  debugging from a workstation) still use http://<radio-host-ip>:<port>
  directly; both ports are opened in UFW via the otbr_rest and
  matter_server entries in ufw_allowed_ports. The Matter server additionally
  gets a no-auth HTTPRoute (matter_server_hostname) so its dashboard and
  commissioning websockets are reachable by a stable LAN name; OTBR gets none
  (its REST API is not a browser UI).

  The device plugin allocates devices into the container at their host path,
  so OTBR's radio URL uses the stable /dev/serial/by-id path directly, at
  460800 baud over the spinel+hdlc protocol (the ZBT-2's serial settings).

  Variables:
    thread_namespace:          Kubernetes namespace where the Thread workloads
                               are deployed.
    device_plugin_image:       Docker image for the generic device plugin.
    device_plugin_image_tag:   Docker image tag for the generic device plugin.
    otbr_image:                Docker image for the OpenThread Border Router.
    otbr_image_tag:            Docker image tag for the OpenThread Border
                               Router.
    otbr_device_path:          Stable /dev/serial/by-id path of the ZBT-2
                               radio on the radio host node.
    otbr_infra_if:             LAN network interface of the radio host that
                               OTBR routes between the Thread mesh and the
                               LAN.
    otbr_rest_port:            Port for the OTBR REST API on the radio host.
    otbr_log_level:            OpenThread log level (0=emergency .. 7=debug).
    otbr_storage_class:        StorageClass for the OTBR state PVC.
    otbr_storage_size:         Size of the OTBR state PVC.
    matter_server_image:       Docker image for the Matter server.
    matter_server_image_tag:   Docker image tag for the Matter server.
    matter_server_port:        Port for the Matter server websocket on the
                               radio host.
    matter_server_hostname:    LAN hostname for the Matter server, exposed via a
                               no-auth HTTPRoute through the traefik-gateway for
                               commissioning.
    matter_server_ble_mode:    How Bluetooth is provided for commissioning —
                               'local' (drive a host Bluetooth adapter via BlueZ
                               over D-Bus, no /ble slot) or 'proxy' (expose /ble
                               for a remote matter-ble-proxy bridge).
    matter_server_uid:         Numeric uid the Matter server container runs as;
                               pinned via runAsUser and matched by the host BlueZ
                               D-Bus policy in local mode.
    matter_apparmor_profile:   Name of the on-host AppArmor profile the Matter
                               server runs under in local mode (docker-default
                               plus a D-Bus allow for org.bluez).
    matter_server_storage_class: StorageClass for the Matter server data PVC.
    matter_server_storage_size:  Size of the Matter server data PVC.
#}
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: otbr-data
  namespace: '{{ thread_namespace }}'
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: otbr
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: '{{ otbr_storage_class }}'
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: '{{ otbr_storage_size }}'
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: generic-device-plugin
  namespace: '{{ thread_namespace }}'
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: generic-device-plugin
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app.kubernetes.io/name: generic-device-plugin
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app.kubernetes.io/name: generic-device-plugin
    spec:
      # Only the radio host has devices to advertise.
      nodeSelector:
        homelab.internal/thread-radio-host: 'true'
      priorityClassName: system-node-critical
      containers:
        - name: generic-device-plugin
          image: '{{ device_plugin_image }}:{{ device_plugin_image_tag }}'
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          args:
            - --domain
            - squat.ai
            # The ZBT-2 radio, advertised by its stable by-id path. count
            # defaults to 1: only one pod may hold the radio at a time.
            - --device
            - '{"name": "zbt2", "groups": [{"paths": [{"path": "{{ otbr_device_path }}"}]}]}'
            # The tun device is shareable; advertise multiple slots so
            # future workloads (e.g. other tunnel users) are not blocked.
            - --device
            - '{"name": "tun", "groups": [{"count": 20, "paths": [{"path": "/dev/net/tun"}]}]}'
          securityContext:
            # Required by the kubelet device-plugin API: the plugin serves
            # gRPC on the kubelet's device-plugins socket and inspects /dev.
            # It is a small single-purpose binary with no network exposure —
            # the privilege deliberately lives here instead of in the OTBR
            # workload container.
            privileged: true
          volumeMounts:
            - name: device-plugins
              mountPath: /var/lib/kubelet/device-plugins
            - name: dev
              mountPath: /dev
      volumes:
        - name: device-plugins
          hostPath:
            path: /var/lib/kubelet/device-plugins
            type: Directory
        - name: dev
          hostPath:
            path: /dev
            type: Directory
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: otbr
  namespace: '{{ thread_namespace }}'
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: otbr
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: otbr
  ports:
    - name: http-rest
      port: {{ otbr_rest_port }}
      targetPort: {{ otbr_rest_port }}
      protocol: TCP
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: otbr
  namespace: '{{ thread_namespace }}'
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: otbr
spec:
  replicas: 1
  strategy:
    type: Recreate
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app.kubernetes.io/name: otbr
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app.kubernetes.io/name: otbr
    spec:
      # Pin to the node holding the ZBT-2 radio (labeled by thread.yml from
      # the thread_radio_host inventory flag).
      nodeSelector:
        homelab.internal/thread-radio-host: 'true'
      # The border router must live in the host network namespace to route
      # between the Thread mesh and the LAN and to advertise itself (mDNS,
      # IPv6 router advertisements) as a real LAN host.
      hostNetwork: true
      containers:
        - name: otbr
          image: '{{ otbr_image }}:{{ otbr_image_tag }}'
          # Built locally on the radio host (no registry) — never pull.
          imagePullPolicy: Never
          securityContext:
            # Unprivileged: the radio and tun devices are granted through
            # the device plugin (resources.limits below), and the container
            # holds only the capabilities OTBR actually uses. IPC_LOCK is for
            # mDNSResponder (mdnsd) memory locking — the same capability the
            # Home Assistant OS OTBR add-on declares. See the file header.
            privileged: false
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            capabilities:
              drop:
                - ALL
              add:
                - NET_ADMIN
                - NET_RAW
                - IPC_LOCK
          resources:
            limits:
              squat.ai/zbt2: 1
              squat.ai/tun: 1
          # OTBR auto-enables border routing at startup but NOT the SRP
          # server's auto-enable mode. Without the SRP server, Matter devices
          # cannot register their operational (_matter._tcp) service, so
          # commissioning reaches the network step and then fails at operational
          # discovery (the border router never advertises the device on the
          # LAN). This mode ties the SRP server to border routing — it enables
          # once border routing is running — and is (re)applied on every start
          # because it is not restored from persisted OpenThread settings. The
          # loop waits for the agent's CLI socket and always exits 0, so a slow
          # agent never fails the hook (a failed postStart kills the container).
          lifecycle:
            postStart:
              exec:
                command:
                  - /bin/sh
                  - -c
                  - >-
                    for i in $(seq 1 30); do
                    ot-ctl srp server auto enable && exit 0;
                    sleep 2;
                    done; exit 0
          env:
            - name: OT_RCP_DEVICE
              value: 'spinel+hdlc+uart://{{ otbr_device_path }}?uart-baudrate=460800'
            - name: OT_INFRA_IF
              value: '{{ otbr_infra_if }}'
            - name: OT_THREAD_IF
              value: wpan0
            - name: OT_LOG_LEVEL
              value: '{{ otbr_log_level }}'
            - name: OT_REST_LISTEN_ADDR
              value: 0.0.0.0
            - name: OT_REST_LISTEN_PORT
              value: '{{ otbr_rest_port }}'
            - name: OT_REST_PORT
              value: '{{ otbr_rest_port }}'
          volumeMounts:
            - name: otbr-data
              mountPath: /data
          readinessProbe:
            tcpSocket:
              port: {{ otbr_rest_port }}
            initialDelaySeconds: 15
            periodSeconds: 10
          livenessProbe:
            tcpSocket:
              port: {{ otbr_rest_port }}
            initialDelaySeconds: 60
            periodSeconds: 20
      volumes:
        - name: otbr-data
          persistentVolumeClaim:
            claimName: otbr-data
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: matter-server-data
  namespace: '{{ thread_namespace }}'
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: matter-server
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: '{{ matter_server_storage_class }}'
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: '{{ matter_server_storage_size }}'
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: matter-server
  namespace: '{{ thread_namespace }}'
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: matter-server
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: matter-server
  ports:
    - name: ws
      port: {{ matter_server_port }}
      targetPort: {{ matter_server_port }}
      protocol: TCP
---
# Exposes the Matter server on the LAN as matter_server_hostname via the
# traefik-gateway, so commissioning (its dashboard, the /ws control API, and
# the /ble commissioning bridge) uses a stable name instead of the radio
# host's IP. No ForwardAuth middleware — the endpoints are websocket/token
# APIs that cannot follow an SSO redirect, and the dashboard is the same one
# already reachable directly on the host port. Home Assistant does NOT use
# this route; it reaches the Matter server by in-cluster Service DNS. The
# HTTPRoute and its backend share this namespace, so no ReferenceGrant is
# needed. ExternalDNS registers the hostname from this route automatically.
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: matter-server
  namespace: '{{ thread_namespace }}'
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: matter-server
  # Read by Homepage's Kubernetes service discovery to build the tile. Written
  # out here rather than picked up from cluster/templates/httproute.yml.j2,
  # which this route does not use because it is rendered as part of this
  # manifest set for ArgoCD rather than applied on its own.
  annotations:
    gethomepage.dev/enabled: 'true'
    gethomepage.dev/name: Matter Server
    gethomepage.dev/group: Home
    gethomepage.dev/icon: matter.png
    gethomepage.dev/description: Matter commissioning dashboard and control API
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - name: traefik-gateway
      namespace: kube-system
      sectionName: https
  hostnames:
    - '{{ matter_server_hostname }}'
  rules:
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
            value: /
      backendRefs:
        - name: matter-server
          namespace: '{{ thread_namespace }}'
          port: {{ matter_server_port }}
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: matter-server
  namespace: '{{ thread_namespace }}'
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: matter-server
spec:
  replicas: 1
  strategy:
    type: Recreate
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app.kubernetes.io/name: matter-server
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app.kubernetes.io/name: matter-server
    spec:
      # Pin to the radio host: routes to the Thread mesh exist directly in
      # its routing table (see the placement note in the file header).
      nodeSelector:
        homelab.internal/thread-radio-host: 'true'
      # The Matter controller commissions and reaches devices via mDNS and
      # IPv6 on the real LAN, which does not work from the pod network.
      hostNetwork: true
      # The matterjs-server image runs as uid/gid 1000 (node). runAsUser pins
      # that uid explicitly so the uid the pod presents to the host D-Bus
      # matches the BlueZ policy installed for local BLE (see service/thread.yml).
      # fsGroup makes the /data PVC group-writable by that user; without it the
      # volume is read-only to node (it was created root-owned by the previous
      # CHIP server generation) and the server crashes with EACCES on /data.
      securityContext:
        runAsUser: {{ matter_server_uid }}
        fsGroup: 1000
      containers:
        - name: matter-server
          image: '{{ matter_server_image }}:{{ matter_server_image_tag }}'
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          env:
            - name: STORAGE_PATH
              value: /data
            - name: PORT
              value: '{{ matter_server_port }}'
            # Pin mDNS to the real LAN interface. With hostNetwork the node
            # exposes dozens of virtual interfaces (cilium, veth) that cannot
            # carry multicast; without this, Matter.js probes them noisily.
            - name: PRIMARY_INTERFACE
              value: '{{ otbr_infra_if }}'
            # Commissioning a factory-new Thread device requires Bluetooth: it
            # has no network credentials yet, so no IP, and can only receive the
            # Thread dataset over BLE. matter_server_ble_mode selects how that
            # Bluetooth is supplied (the two are mutually exclusive in the
            # server); both commissioning workflows are in docs/home-automation.md.
{% if matter_server_ble_mode == 'proxy' %}
            # proxy: expose a /ble websocket that a remote matter-ble-proxy
            # client bridges to an off-cluster adapter (any LAN machine near the
            # device). Use when the radio host has no Bluetooth of its own. Home
            # Assistant grabs the single /ble slot on startup, so it must be
            # scaled down during commissioning (see the docs).
            - name: BLE_PROXY
              value: 'true'
{% else %}
            # local: drive a Bluetooth adapter on the radio host directly, so no
            # /ble slot exists (nothing for Home Assistant to occupy) and no
            # external bridge is needed. NOBLE_BINDINGS=dbus makes the noble
            # library reach the host's BlueZ over the mounted D-Bus socket, which
            # keeps this container unprivileged (the raw-HCI path would need
            # NET_ADMIN/NET_RAW). BLUETOOTH_ADAPTER is the HCI index (0 = hci0).
            # Requires bluez plus the D-Bus policy that service/thread.yml
            # installs on the radio host, both gated on this same mode.
            - name: BLUETOOTH_ADAPTER
              value: '0'
            - name: NOBLE_BINDINGS
              value: dbus
            - name: DBUS_SYSTEM_BUS_ADDRESS
              value: unix:path=/run/dbus/system_bus_socket
{% endif %}
            # Force the dashboard into reverse-proxy ("production") mode so it
            # auto-connects its websocket to the current page URL
            # (wss://matter_server_hostname/ws through the gateway) instead of
            # prompting for a server URL. The dashboard otherwise infers this
            # only when served on the backend's own port; behind the HTTPRoute
            # it is served on 443, so without this it prompts on every visit.
            - name: PRODUCTION_MODE
              value: 'true'
          securityContext:
            # Unprivileged: Matter.js mDNS and websocket are ordinary sockets
            # (>1024), and its mDNS sets SO_REUSEPORT so it port-shares UDP
            # 5353 with the OTBR mDNSResponder on the same host. hostNetwork
            # alone is sufficient — no added capabilities.
            privileged: false
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            capabilities:
              drop:
                - ALL
{% if matter_server_ble_mode == 'local' %}
            # Local BLE reaches the host BlueZ over D-Bus, but the docker-default
            # AppArmor profile Kubernetes applies BLOCKS D-Bus — the container's
            # first D-Bus call (Hello) is denied and the server crashes (write
            # EPIPE). Rather than dropping confinement entirely (Unconfined), this
            # references a custom on-host profile that IS docker-default plus a
            # single allow for D-Bus to org.bluez, so every other restriction
            # stays in force. service/thread.yml installs and loads that profile
            # on the radio host; it must be loaded there or the pod won't start.
            # (proxy mode keeps the stock docker-default — no override here.)
            appArmorProfile:
              type: Localhost
              localhostProfile: '{{ matter_apparmor_profile }}'
{% endif %}
          volumeMounts:
            - name: matter-server-data
              mountPath: /data
{% if matter_server_ble_mode == 'local' %}
            # Host D-Bus system socket, so noble's D-Bus binding can reach the
            # host BlueZ for local Bluetooth. Writable, not read-only: a
            # read-only mount is reported to break the D-Bus connection
            # (broken-pipe errors), and access is governed by the host BlueZ
            # D-Bus policy for this container's uid regardless.
            - name: dbus-system
              mountPath: /run/dbus
              readOnly: false
{% endif %}
          readinessProbe:
            tcpSocket:
              port: {{ matter_server_port }}
            initialDelaySeconds: 10
            periodSeconds: 10
          livenessProbe:
            tcpSocket:
              port: {{ matter_server_port }}
            initialDelaySeconds: 60
            periodSeconds: 20
      volumes:
        - name: matter-server-data
          persistentVolumeClaim:
            claimName: matter-server-data
{% if matter_server_ble_mode == 'local' %}
        - name: dbus-system
          hostPath:
            path: /run/dbus
            type: Directory
{% endif %}
